Experiment: Setup and Configuration of LAN Using a Switch

Aim
To set up and configure a Local Area Network (LAN) using a switch and Ethernet cables, assign IP addresses to host machines, test connectivity using the ping command, and share files and folders between computers.

Requirements
- 3–4 computers or host machines
- One Ethernet switch
- Ethernet (UTP) cables
- Windows operating system

Algorithm / Procedure
1. Connect the Computers
1. Connect 3–4 computers to the switch using Ethernet cables.
2. Ensure that the link lights on the switch and network interfaces are active.
3. Verify that all computers are connected to the same switch.
2. Assign IP Addresses
1. Open Control Panel → Network and Sharing Center → Change adapter settings.
2. Right-click Ethernet and select Properties.
3. Select Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4) and click Properties.
4. Select Use the following IP address and configure each computer as shown below.
Computer	IP address	Subnet mask
PC1	192.168.1.1	255.255.255.0
PC2	192.168.1.2	255.255.255.0
PC3	192.168.1.3	255.255.255.0
PC4	192.168.1.4	255.255.255.0
Leave the default gateway blank if the LAN is isolated and does not require internet access.
3. Check Connectivity Using Ping
1. Open Command Prompt on PC1.
2. Enter the following command:
ping 192.168.1.2


3. Repeat the test for the other computers:
ping 192.168.1.3
ping 192.168.1.4


4. Verify that replies are received without packet loss.

Sample output:
C:\> ping 192.168.1.2

Reply from 192.168.1.2: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
Reply from 192.168.1.2: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
Reply from 192.168.1.2: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
Reply from 192.168.1.2: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128

Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 4, Lost = 0 (0% loss)


Note: This is an illustrative output. If ping fails, check the IP addresses, cable connections, and firewall settings.
4. Share and Access Files and Folders
1. Create a folder named LANShare on PC1.
2. Right-click the folder and select Properties → Sharing → Advanced Sharing.
3. Enable Share this folder and assign suitable permissions to the intended users.
4. Ensure that Network Discovery and File and Printer Sharing are enabled for the appropriate private network profile.
5. On PC2, open File Explorer and enter the following path in the address bar:
\\192.168.1.1\LANShare


6. If prompted, enter the credentials of an authorized account on PC1.
7. Create or copy a test file into the shared folder and verify that it can be accessed from PC2.

Expected output:
Folder shared successfully.
PC2 accessed \\192.168.1.1\LANShare
File transferred successfully.

Result
A LAN was configured using a switch and Ethernet cables. IP addresses were assigned to the host computers, connectivity was verified using the ping command, and file and folder sharing between computers was demonstrated.
